<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="fundamental-goal" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Fundamental Goal: Personal Development &amp; Growth</title>

  <p>
    The center of the diagram represents the personal growth of the students, and is centrally
    located as that is the fundamental, or ultimate, goal of the program. Accordingly, the six
    dimensions all flow to the middle as these dimensions act as guides for what the program needs
    to do to facilitate student growth. Around personal development are three rings: program
    structure, pedagogical practices, and curriculum. These represent the three contexts in which
    we implement the six dimensions when building a culturally responsive program. An effective
    program that supports student growth is one that embraces all six dimensions and carefully
    implements them in each of the three contexts.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this program, a particular way to promote personal development is by connecting with the
    goal of <em>going to college</em>. Since the program runs on a university campus, students
    see what a university looks like and how it runs. Mentors are undergraduate students who can
    share information about their classes and other aspects of being a university student. The
    goal is not to push students to choose a college-bound path, but instead encourage them that
    they are capable of choosing that path if they want, and help start developing it.
  </p>

</section>
